@node	0.2, 0.3, 0.1, Chapter 0
@section Functions, recursion, and iteration

A Rust function is a named, typed procedure:

@example rust
fn square(n: i64) -> i64 @{
    n * n
@}
@end example

The last expression in the body, with no semicolon, is the return value; an
explicit @code{return} works too and is normal for an early exit. @code{if}
is an expression, not a statement, so it can appear anywhere a value is
expected:

@example rust
let n = -3;
let sign = if n < 0 @{ -1 @} else if n > 0 @{ 1 @} else @{ 0 @};
println!("@{sign@}");
// => -1
@end example

Both arms of an @code{if} used this way must produce the same type; the
compiler checks it, the way it checks everything else about the value an
expression produces.

Rust has three looping constructs, and no special form for tail-recursive
counting the way the book's @code{do} loops or named-@code{let} would.
@code{while} repeats for as long as a condition holds, checked before each
pass; @code{loop} repeats unconditionally until a @code{break}, which can
hand back a value from the loop; @code{for} walks an iterator (@ref{0.5}
covers what counts as one), most often a range like @code{1..=5}.

@example rust
let mut n = 10;
let mut halvings = 0;
while n > 1 @{
    n /= 2;
    halvings += 1;
@}
println!("@{halvings@}");
// => 3

let mut count = 0;
let first_over_20 = loop @{
    count += 3;
    if count > 20 @{
        break count;
    @}
@};
println!("@{first_over_20@}");
// => 21

let mut total = 0;
for i in 1..=5 @{
    total += i;
@}
println!("@{total@}");
// => 15
@end example

All three coexist as plain control flow, not syntax the way @code{if} is; the
runnable listing above is @code{examples/sec_0_2_loops.rs}.

@subheading Recursion, iteration, and the call stack

The book's recurring theme in this section is that Scheme's tail-call
optimization has no Rust equivalent. A recursive Rust function keeps one
stack frame alive for every call still waiting on a result, exactly like a
recursive @newterm{process} in the book's sense (1.2.1) whether or not the
recursive call is the last thing the function does:

@example rust
fn sum_to_recursive(n: u64) -> u64 @{
    if n == 0 @{ 0 @} else @{ n + sum_to_recursive(n - 1) @}
@}

fn sum_to_iterative(n: u64) -> u64 @{
    let mut total = 0;
    for i in 1..=n @{
        total += i;
    @}
    total
@}

let by_recursion = sum_to_recursive(100);
println!("@{by_recursion@}");
// => 5050

let by_loop = sum_to_iterative(100);
println!("@{by_loop@}");
// => 5050
@end example

Both compute the same sum, and both are correct Rust; the difference the book
cares about is the shape of the process each one runs. The recursive version
builds a chain of @code{n} pending additions before it can produce anything,
one stack frame per pending addition; large enough @code{n} overflows the
call stack (a hard crash, not a checked error) long before @code{u64} would.
The iterative version updates one @code{total} in a single frame for however
many iterations @code{for} runs. Section @ref{1.2} rewrites this exact
argument for the book's own recursive procedures, with @newterm{recursion
depth} and iterations counted side by side; the accompanying exercise below
does it once here, on simpler ground, with a process the book already uses in
1.2.2.

The runnable listing above is
@code{examples/sec_0_2_recursion_vs_iteration.rs}.

@subheading Exercises

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.1}Exercise 0.1:} Write a recursive @code{factorial}
that computes @math{n!} using @code{checked_mul} (@ref{0.1}), returning
@code{None} instead of wrapping when the exact result would overflow
@code{i128}. Then find the largest @code{n} for which your @code{factorial}
still returns @code{Some}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.2}Exercise 0.2:} Section 1.2.2 counts ways to make
change from a fixed set of coins by a recursive process: use the largest
kind of coin available, or don't. Write @code{count_change_recursive} that
way for United States coins (half-dollars, quarters, dimes, nickels,
pennies), then write @code{count_change_iterative} as a loop over an
accumulator table indexed by amount, one pass per denomination. Instrument
the recursive version to report the maximum call depth it reaches, and
compare that depth against the loop's for amounts up to 400 cents.
@end quotation

Scaffolds for both live in @code{exercises/ch00/sec_0_2.rs}; reference
solutions with passing tests are in @code{solutions/ch00/sec_0_2.rs}, with
the reasoning in @code{solutions/ch00/ex_0_01.md} and
@code{solutions/ch00/ex_0_02.md}.
